Chapter: ProcessCaps
  cites Verify chapter VerifiedLoader

 Stage 4 of the capability wiring: the verified loader's grant
 bitmask becomes kernel authority. The kernel keeps one capability
 word per process at offset 56 of its 256-byte process-table entry
 (table base 20480); the syscall layer bit-tests that word against
 the current process. These functions let OS code that spawns a
 verified binary write the LoadDecision's grant into the slot the
 new process will run under, and read it back for audit. A denied
 decision writes nothing and reports False - the caller must not
 start the process.

Section: Process Table

  proc-table-base-addr : Integer = 20480

  proc-entry-bytes : Integer = 256

  proc-cap-word-offset : Integer = 56

  proc-cap-addr : Integer -> Integer
  proc-cap-addr (pid) = proc-table-base-addr + pid * proc-entry-bytes + proc-cap-word-offset

Section: Grant Wiring

 A grant has three parts and the process table has a slot for each: the
 capability word at offset 56 says what the process may do, the
 filesystem scope at offset 64 says what path it may name, and the
 network scope at offset 24 says what authority it may reach. Writing
 the capability word without a scope hands a loaded binary its authority
 and loses the limit on it -- a program whose manifest said "filesystem
 read, under /config/" would run with filesystem read over the whole
 volume, and one that said "network write, to api.example.com" would run
 able to POST anywhere. So apply-load-decision writes ALL THREE, and a
 denied decision writes NONE.

 Each scope goes through its process-set-*-scope helper rather than a
 poke because a Text is a pointer and the process table wants that
 pointer, not its bytes; the helpers are privileged
 (cap-capability-admin), which is the point -- installing a scope is the
 loader's business and not an application's.

  write-process-grant : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  write-process-grant (pid) (mask) =
   let lo = poke-32 (proc-cap-addr pid) 0 mask
   in poke-32 (proc-cap-addr pid) 4 0

  read-process-grant : Integer -> Integer
  read-process-grant (pid) = peek-32 (proc-cap-addr pid) 0

  write-process-scope : Integer, Text -> [Capability] Integer
  write-process-scope (pid) (scope) = process-set-scope pid scope

  read-process-scope : Integer -> Text
  read-process-scope (pid) = process-get-scope pid

  write-process-network-scope : Integer, Text -> [Capability] Integer
  write-process-network-scope (pid) (scope) = process-set-network-scope pid scope

  read-process-network-scope : Integer -> Text
  read-process-network-scope (pid) = process-get-network-scope pid

 The bare `True` that used to end this act block cannot come back: a
 literal on its own line after an act-bind is parsed as an ARGUMENT to
 the line above it (CDX1070), not as the block's result. The block ends
 in an application instead, and wired-ok is what makes that application
 an honest one.

  apply-load-decision : Integer, LoadDecision -> [Capability] Boolean
  apply-load-decision (pid) (d) =
   when d
    is LoadApproved (g) -> act
      s <- write-process-scope pid (g.scope)
      n <- write-process-network-scope pid (g.net-scope)
      wired-ok (write-process-grant pid (g.bitmask))
    end
    is LoadDenied (r) -> False

  wired-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  wired-ok (written) = True
